          Fragment:md
            ## Installation

            nmbl integrates with Astro through an official integration:

          
          InstallWidget(packages="@nmbl-lang/astro")
          Fragment:md
            ## Configuration

            Add the integration to your `astro.config.mjs`:

            ```js title="astro.config.mjs"
            import { defineConfig } from 'astro/config';
            import nmbl from '@nmbl-lang/astro';

            export default defineConfig({
              integrations: [nmbl()],
            });
            ```

            That's the entire setup — no Vite or TypeScript config changes are needed.

            ## Basic Usage

            Add a `<template lang="nmbl">` block to any `.astro` page or component, then write
            the markup as nmbl. Variables from the component frontmatter (the `---` fence) are
            available with Astro's `{expr}` interpolation:

            ```astro title="src/pages/index.astro"
            ---
            const title = 'My Site';
            const items = ['Apple', 'Banana', 'Cherry'];
            ---

            <template lang="nmbl">
            html(lang="en")
              head
                meta(charset="utf-8")
                title {title}
              body
                div#app
                  h1 {title}
                  ul
                    @each(item, i of items)
                      li {i + 1}. {item}
            </template>
            ```

            Interpolation uses Astro's `{expr}` syntax. Frontmatter variables and `Astro.*`
            globals are accessible, and regular Astro/JSX-style attributes pass straight through.

            ## Control Flow

            nmbl's `@if` / `@elseif` / `@else` and `@each` blocks compile to the JSX-style
            expressions Astro uses:

            ```nmbl framework=astro
            @if(user)
              p Welcome back, {user.name}
            @else
              a(href="/login") Log in

            ul
              @each(item, i of items)
                li {i + 1}. {item}
            ```

            `@if(cond)` compiles to `{cond ? (…) : (…)}` and `@each(item of items)` compiles
            to `{items.map((item, i) => (…))}`. One Astro/JSX-specific rule worth knowing: when
            you use `:key`, the loop body must have a single root element (the key goes on it) —
            a multi-root `@each` body with `:key` is a compile error. Conditions and loop
            expressions pass straight through, so you write them exactly as you would in Astro.

            ## Markdown Blocks

            The `@nmbl-lang/astro` integration renders `:md` content blocks through your
            project's own Astro Markdown pipeline — the same remark/rehype plugins and syntax
            highlighting that `.md` files get. Drop prose-heavy content inline with
            `div.prose:md` (or `Fragment:md`):

            ```nmbl framework=astro
            article.prose:md
              ## Getting Started

              Write **markdown** with [links](/docs), lists, and fenced code —
              it renders with the same pipeline your `.md` files use.

              - point one
              - point two
            ```

            `{expr}` interpolation works in `:md` prose text too, but components don't (the
            markdown layer can't represent them) — drop to nmbl siblings for components.

            ## Comments

            `//` comments are stripped from the output (dev notes); `//!` comments are rendered
            as real HTML comments:

            ```nmbl framework=astro
            // dev note — never shipped
            //! becomes <!-- an HTML comment -->
            p Visible text
            ```

            You can also comment out individual props inside a tag's parens — handy while
            debugging:

            ```nmbl framework=astro
            button(
              type="submit"
              // disabled
              /* aria-label="Save" */
            ) Save
            ```

            ## Editor Support

            Astro editor support comes from `@nmbl-lang/language-server` (a Volar 2.4 server)
            wired through the nmbl VS Code extension. It activates only on `.astro` files that
            contain a `<template lang="nmbl">` block — other files are left untouched.

          .features-grid
            .feature
              h4 🎨 Syntax Highlighting
              p nmbl syntax is highlighted inside template lang="nmbl" blocks
            .feature
              h4 💡 HTML Completions
              p Tag names, attribute names, and values are suggested via the embedded HTML language service
            .feature
              h4 🔗 Go to Definition
              p Tokens map back to your nmbl source, so navigation works on the original file
            .feature
              h4 ❗ Syntax Diagnostics
              p nmbl syntax errors and unsupported control-flow blocks are surfaced inline

          Fragment:md
            > **Note:** Deep, framework-type-aware checking of expressions inside `@if` / `@each`
            > is not yet available for Astro — the Astro Language Server has no plugin hook for
            > injecting a second virtual language service, so expressions compile correctly but
            > aren't type-checked. For the fullest type intellisense today, Vue is the most
            > complete via its dedicated Volar plugin.

            ## Troubleshooting

          .troubleshooting:md
            ### Template not rendering

            Make sure `nmbl()` is in the `integrations` array of your `astro.config.mjs`, and
            restart the dev server after editing the config.

            ### Syntax errors with indentation

            nmbl uses indentation for structure. Your root element must start at column 0 with
            no leading whitespace, and use consistent 2-space indentation (no tabs).

            ### Markdown block not highlighting code

            Fenced code inside `:md` blocks uses your Astro Markdown highlighter (e.g. Shiki or
            expressive-code). Make sure your highlighter is configured as usual in
            `astro.config.mjs` — `:md` reuses it automatically.

